This project builds on two upstream works. Neither is redistributed in full;
`vendor/` is not tracked.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCICompanion -- https://github.com/icefallgames/SCICompanion  (scicompanion.com)
Copyright (c) 2015 Philip Fortier
GPL-2.0-or-later

`tools/scicompile/` is a headless Linux port of SCICompanion's SCI script
compiler. It contains modified copies of SCICompanion sources (each file states
that it is a modified copy and what changed) and links its compiler, class
browser, resource map and SCO subsystems. It is therefore a derivative work and
is licensed GPL-2.0-or-later; see tools/scicompile/LICENSE.

Changes made:
  * headless Linux build: MFC/Win32 replaced by minimal shims (tools/scicompile/compat)
  * mechanical MSVC -> GCC fixes (dependent-name typename, rvalue stream binding, ...)
  * ScriptId::_Init path fix: split on the last separator of EITHER kind, so POSIX
    '/' paths resolve (previously broke the class browser and Compile-All)
  * new `--all` (Compile-All) and `--sco` modes
  * two upstream defect fixes in SCO.cpp, both triggered only by compiling a
    DIFFERENT decompiler's output:
      - SCOFromScriptAndCompiledScript: nameToCompiledObject[...] used operator[],
        which inserts a nullptr for an absent class name and is then dereferenced
      - the export loop bounds-checked the instance branch but not the procedure
        branch, so a procedure-classified export with no public procedures in
        source indexed off the end of the vector

Prof-UIS (bundled with SCICompanion) is proprietary and is neither used nor
redistributed here; the port is headless and links no GUI code.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
sci-tools -- https://github.com/sluicebox/sci-tools
Copyright (c) sluicebox
MIT

Used as the decompiler front-end, via our MIT fork
https://github.com/katiahayati/sci-tools (branch `json-ir`), which adds a `--json`
flag serialising the typed control-flow AST it already builds -- so the analysis
can consume it instead of reparsing generated text -- plus each script's export
table. Both changes are additive: without `--json` the decompiler behaves as
upstream. The fork is cloned at build time, not vendored into this repository.

